.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_SIGNALS 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_sigfillset
.Nd signal set helpers and signal number constants
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signals.h
.Ft t_sigset
.Fn xft_sigfillset void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In signals.h
provides an opaque signal set type and a single helper that fills it.
The signal numbers, the operation constants for
.Xr xft_sigprocmask 3 ,
and the size of the set all come from this header.
.Ss Types and constants
The set type is defined in
.In types/signal_types.h .
In a freestanding build on x86_64 or aarch64 it is an 8-byte aligned 64-bit
word, and
.Dv XFT_SIGSET_SIZE
is 8.
Otherwise
.Vt t_sigset
is
.Vt sigset_t
from the platform's
.In signal.h .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#define XFT_SIGSET_SIZE 8

/* freestanding x86_64 / aarch64 */
typedef t_u64a t_sigset;

/* libc, or any other architecture */
# include <signal.h>
typedef sigset_t t_sigset;
.Ed
.Pp
The operation flags passed to
.Xr xft_sigprocmask 3
are
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#define XFT_BLOCK    0
#define XFT_UNBLOCK  1
#define XFT_SETMASK  2
.Ed
.Pp
On a freestanding build the signal numbers are defined by the header
itself, using the Linux values:
.Dv XFT_SIGHUP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGINT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGQUIT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGILL ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTRAP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGABRT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGBUS ,
.Dv XFT_SIGFPE ,
.Dv XFT_SIGKILL ,
.Dv XFT_SIGUSR1 ,
.Dv XFT_SIGSEGV ,
.Dv XFT_SIGUSR2 ,
.Dv XFT_SIGPIPE ,
.Dv XFT_SIGALRM ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTERM ,
.Dv XFT_SIGSTKFLT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGCHLD ,
.Dv XFT_SIGCONT ,
.Dv XFT_SIGSTOP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTSTP ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTTIN ,
.Dv XFT_SIGTTOU ,
.Dv XFT_SIGURG ,
.Dv XFT_SIGXCPU ,
.Dv XFT_SIGXFSZ ,
.Dv XFT_SIGVTALRM ,
.Dv XFT_SIGPROF ,
.Dv XFT_SIGWINCH ,
.Dv XFT_SIGIO ,
.Dv XFT_SIGPWR
and
.Dv XFT_SIGSYS .
On a hosted build they are aliases for the platform's own
.Dv SIG*
constants; the library still exposes the
.Ql XFT_
names so callers do not have to include
.In signal.h
themselves.
.Ss Filling a set
.Fn xft_sigfillset
returns a set with every bit set, i.e.
.Ql sizeof(t_sigset)
bytes of
.Ql 0xff .
It is
.Ql const
and does not touch any global state.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_sigfillset
returns the filled set.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Block every signal, then restore the previous mask.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

void	block_everything(void)
{
	t_sigset	all;
	t_sigset	old;

	all = xft_sigfillset();
	xft_sigprocmask(XFT_SETMASK, &all, &old);
	/* ... */
	xft_sigprocmask(XFT_SETMASK, &old, nullptr);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_rt 3 ,
.Xr xft_syscalls 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The
.Vt t_sigset
type differs between builds.
On a freestanding x86_64 or aarch64 build it is exactly 8 bytes; the libc
.Vt sigset_t
on Linux is 128 bytes.
Code that assumes one or the other will not link, or will silently truncate
the mask, when the build flavor changes.
.Pp
Only the signal numbers listed above are defined by the header.
Any other signal, real-time signal, or the platform's
.Dv SIGRTMIN
through
.Dv SIGRTMAX
range, must be named numerically.
.Pp
.Fn xft_sigfillset
sets every bit, including bits that do not correspond to any signal the
kernel can deliver.
Passing the result to
.Xr xft_sigprocmask 3
is valid, but the kernel will ignore the reserved bits.
